                <section class="section" id="the-train">
                    <h2 class="title">The Train</h2>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>
                            Merging into <code>main</code> never publishes to nuget.org.
                            Every merge that touches <code>src/**</code> runs the <code>deploy_main</code> workflow, which builds,
                            packs, and pushes the packages to the private GitHub package feed (dogfoodable at any time),
                            and then maintains the <strong>draft release</strong> for the version tag GitVersion computed
                            for the current head of <code>main</code>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The only ship event is the maintainer <strong>publishing that draft release</strong>: one click
                            per finished milestone. Publishing triggers <code>deploy_release</code> (the nuget.org push)
                            and <code>deploy_rforge_docs</code> (this site going live).
                            Nothing in the pipeline publishes on its own.
                        </p>
                        <div class="notification">
                            <strong>Consumers:</strong> GitHub feed packages are current with every merge.
                            nuget.org and this docs site always reflect the last <em>published</em> release.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="section" id="one-draft-per-version">
                    <h2 class="title">One Draft Per Version</h2>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>
                            Each merge refreshes the draft release for the pending version so its notes cover everything
                            merged so far. Before issue <a href="https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/issues/119">#119</a>,
                            the workflow called <code>gh release create</code> on every merge, so successive merges stacked
                            duplicate drafts under the same version tag, each with a different (and stale) changelog.
                            That is fixed: <code>deploy_main</code> now keeps <strong>exactly one draft per version tag</strong>,
                            maintained by <a href="https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/.github/scripts/draft-release.sh"><code>draft-release.sh</code></a>
                            (PR <a href="https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/pull/124">#124</a>).
                        </p>
                        <p>Each run handles four cases:</p>
                        <table class="table is-striped is-fullwidth">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>State for the version tag</th>
                                    <th>What the run does</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>No release exists</td>
                                    <td>Creates a draft release with auto-generated notes, wrapped in the auto markers.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>A draft already exists</td>
                                    <td>Refreshes it in place: notes are regenerated for the new head of <code>main</code> and only the auto region is rewritten. Everything outside the markers is kept verbatim.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Several drafts stacked (legacy duplicates from before the fix)</td>
                                    <td>Keeps the newest draft, absorbs the older drafts' hand-written text under a <code>## Preserved from duplicate draft</code> heading so nothing is lost, and deletes the extras.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>A published release exists for the tag</td>
                                    <td>Leaves it untouched. Any stray draft for that version is swept. A published release is never edited or unpublished by the pipeline.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p>
                            The maintenance logic is plain shell on top of <code>gh</code> (no third-party actions), and it is
                            pinned by a runnable acceptance harness in the repo:
                            <code>bash .github/scripts/tests/test-draft-release.sh</code> (14 scenarios, including the
                            original live incident).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="section" id="draft-notes">
                    <h2 class="title">Draft Notes and the Auto Region</h2>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>
                            The draft body is split by two HTML comments into a bot-owned region and a human-owned region:
                        </p>
                        <pre><code>&lt;!-- auto-release-notes:start --&gt;
(auto-generated changelog: merged PRs since the previous release,
 plus the Full Changelog compare link; regenerated on every merge)
&lt;!-- auto-release-notes:end --&gt;

(your scratch text lives outside the markers and is never deleted)</code></pre>
                        <p>
                            Inside the markers is the bot's: it is overwritten on every subsequent merge, so do not edit it
                            by hand. Outside the markers is the maintainer's scratch space: the refresh never deletes text,
                            so release notes you type around the auto region survive every refresh.
                            Drafts created before the markers existed are preserved wholesale as human text.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="section" id="publishing">
                    <h2 class="title">Publishing</h2>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>
                            When a milestone is done, open the single draft release for the version. Its notes now list
                            everything merged so far, plus any scratch text, so there is nothing to reconcile between
                            duplicates. Publishing it ships everything at once: the GitHub Release for the tag, the
                            nuget.org push, and the docs site update.
                        </p>
                        <div class="notification is-warning">
                            <strong>Maintainers:</strong> publishing is a deliberate, one-click act per milestone.
                            Do not create releases by hand for unreleased version tags; the next merge will collide with them.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="section" id="draft-list">
                    <h2 class="title">Reading the Draft List</h2>
                    <div class="content">
                        <p>
                            Two GitHub quirks to know when you run <code>gh release list</code> or browse releases:
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                A draft whose tag has not been published yet is not resolvable by tag, and GitHub rewrites
                                its tag name to an <code>untagged-</code> placeholder shortly after creation.
                                The version number survives in the draft's <em>name</em>, so look drafts up by name, not by tag.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                If you ever see multiple drafts for one version (leftovers from before the fix), no manual
                                cleanup is needed: the next <code>deploy_main</code> run collapses them to one, preserving
                                all hand-written text.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
